"""Literal 1:1 SVG template for the PnL card, transcribed from the user's
Figma export (``81c8fd0c-pnl_card.html``, 1280x800).

Every geometric shape, gradient, and wordmark glyph in that export is an
outlined static ``<path>`` — this module reproduces every one of them
unchanged, translated from the original HTML's ``<div style="left:Xpx;
top:Ypx"><svg>...</svg></div>`` wrapper layout into ``<g transform="translate(X,
Y)">`` groups so the whole thing is a single valid SVG document cairosvg can
rasterize directly.

Every ``style="fill:X;fill:color(display-p3 ...);fill-opacity:1"`` attribute
from the export is dropped (verified via a minimal repro: cairosvg renders a
shape with that style attribute as solid black instead of its fill color,
since it can't parse the CSS Color-4 ``color(display-p3 ...)`` function and
that becomes the winning ``fill`` declaration). Every path already carries
the equivalent color via its ``fill="..."`` presentation attribute, so
nothing is lost — this is a rendering-compatibility fix, not a data change.

Text is rendered in the real "Space Grotesk" font (bundled at
``report/fonts/SpaceGrotesk-VariableFont_wght.ttf``, registered with
fontconfig in-process by ``report/_common.py::_register_bundled_fonts()``)
to match the export exactly — cairosvg does not parse ``@font-face`` rules,
so relying on font-family alone requires fontconfig to know about the file.

Only 6 placeholders in the original export were ever meant to carry live
data — every other pixel is static decoration or branding:
    {pnl_usd}      big headline PnL figure (e.g. "+$123.45")
    {pnl_perc}     PnL percentage (e.g. "+2.34%")
    {pnl_color}    fill color for the two above — the one deviation from the
                   literal export (which hardcodes #05DF72): green when the
                   underlying value is >= 0, red when negative
    {timeframe}    label for the headline figure (e.g. "Daily")
    {yoy_chg}      lifetime/overall PnL percentage (e.g. "+12.3%")
    {yoy_pos}      realized/closed position count
    {username}     member display name

The pnl_usd/pnl_perc divs declare ``text-align: right`` but no explicit
width, so in the original HTML they shrink-wrap to their content and
text-align has no visible effect — rendered as left-anchored text starting
at the div's "left" position, same as every other text element here.

pnl_perc is positioned flush below pnl_usd's own line box (top 291 +
line-height 106.42 = 397.42) rather than at its exported top of 360.73 — the
one deliberate position deviation from the literal export. Confirmed by
rendering with the real Space Grotesk font: at the exported coordinates the
two texts still visually collide regardless of font, since pnl_usd's ink
naturally extends close to y=360 at that font-size/position for any
realistic PnL string.
"""

